:title("Kotlin (subset) interpreter") ;
:description("Parses Kotlin and executes it directly, without generating code.

Covered: top level and local functions with block or expression bodies, parameter
DEFAULT values (evaluated in the callee frame, so a default may read an earlier
parameter), NAMED arguments, `vararg` parameters and the `*spread` argument,
local functions and anonymous functions (fun(x: Int) = ...), lambdas with real
closures, multi statement bodies, destructuring parameters ({ (a, b) -> ... }),
`it`, explicit labels (lab@{ ... }) and lambdas WITH RECEIVER (val f: Int.(Int) -> Int),
trailing lambda call syntax, and callable references (::fn, recv::member).
Control flow: if/when as statement and as expression (subject, subjectless,
`when (val x = e)`, comma separated values, `in`/`!in` ranges, `is`/`!is` types),
while/do/for, break/continue including labelled forms, `return` (a bare return
inside a lambda is Kotlin's NON-LOCAL return out of the enclosing function) and
return@label. RANGES are first class values - a..b, a..<b, a until b, a downTo b,
any of them `step k`, over Ints or Chars - so they iterate, answer `x in r`, and can
be stored. Char is its own boxed value: it prints as the glyph and compares and does
arithmetic as its code. Exceptions: throw/try/catch/finally as STATEMENTS and as
EXPRESSIONS (a try expression's value is the last expression of whichever block ran),
with the catch clauses discriminated by the DECLARED exception type in both forms
(excTryTyped, languages/lib); the builtin Throwable hierarchy provides `message`.

Types: classes with primary constructor properties, SECONDARY constructors (selected
by argument count), `init` blocks and property initializers in declaration order,
property ACCESSORS (get()/set(v) with the backing `field`), INHERITANCE with
constructor arguments and `super.m()`, INTERFACES with default methods, interface
implementation BY DELEGATION (: I by impl), abstract/open/sealed (parsed; the model
is dynamic), DATA classes and @JvmInline VALUE classes (componentN, copy with named
arguments, structural ==, the generated toString), ENUM classes (entries as
singletons with name/ordinal, constructor arguments, per-entry bodies, members, and
unqualified entry access inside the enum), OBJECT declarations and object
expressions, COMPANION objects (named or not; ClassName.member forwards to the
singleton), NESTED and INNER classes (this@Outer walks the enclosing instances),
annotation classes, and `typealias`. GENERICS are parsed and erased, including
variance, `where` constraints and star projections - except that a `reified` type
parameter is genuine: an explicit type argument at the call site resolves `x is T`.
OPERATOR CONVENTIONS dispatch to the class: plus/minus/times/div/rem, unaryMinus,
compareTo, contains, get/set, invoke, componentN, equals - and a user `infix fun`
is callable as `a name b`. EXTENSIONS: extension functions and extension PROPERTIES at top
level, locally, and as MEMBERS of a class - a member extension binds the extension
receiver to `this` and keeps the enclosing instance as a second implicit receiver, so
both are readable unqualified and through this@Outer. All of them dispatch by NAME
(the receiver type is dropped, so an extension must not reuse a builtin or member
name). DESTRUCTURING declarations, destructuring assignments and destructuring
for-loops go through componentN, in the parenthesized and the bracket spelling.
Assignment to a COMPUTED target is genuine - foo(x).field = y, bag().arr[i] += 1 -
and a compound assignment onto a call result goes through plusAssign and its siblings.

DELEGATED PROPERTIES follow the real protocol: `by lazy { init }` is special-cased
(eager - the initializer runs once at the declaration, not on first access), and every
other delegate is stored and asked, so each read calls its getValue(thisRef, property)
and each write its setValue(thisRef, property, value). That holds for a member, a
top-level property and a local one alike; the property reference passed as `property`
answers .name. An expression that is not a delegate at all - it has no getValue - is
reported as not implemented when the declaration runs. An EXTENSION delegated property
has no single owner to pass as thisRef and stays unsupported.

The value model is untyped: Int arithmetic is 32 bit and / truncates, + concatenates
when one side is a String, and == is structural for strings, Chars, data and value
classes and identity otherwise; hashCode() is the matching structural hash (java's:
the 31-fold for a list, the SUM for a set, doubleToLongBits for a Double), so equal
values hash equally. A SET is its own shape - it deduplicates, compares without regard
to order and is never equal to a List - while an ARRAY still collapses onto a list.
The builtins are println/print/abs/max/min/exitProcess, listOf and its aliases, and
the list methods map/filter/sumOf/forEach/count/any/joinToString plus the usual
size/get/add/contains. A qualified stdlib path (kotlin.math.abs) resolves to the same
builtin as the bare name.

COROUTINES run SYNCHRONOUSLY. runBlocking, coroutineScope, supervisorScope and
withContext are their bodies; async and launch run their block at the point it is
written and answer a Job whose await/join are immediate; delay and yield are no-ops;
Dispatchers.IO and friends are names that carry no behaviour. So the RESULTS are right
and the ORDERING is not: nothing suspends, so two coroutines never interleave and a
program whose output depends on interleaving prints a different (but deterministic)
order than kotlinx.coroutines. Flow, Channel, select and cancellation are not
modelled. `suspend` itself remains syntax only.

A SEQUENCE IS EAGER: sequenceOf is listOf and every operator on it is the list
operator, so a pipeline over a finite source gives Kotlin's answer while an INFINITE
generateSequence is materialised up to a cap of 100000 elements (the cap prints a
warning when it is reached) rather than being pulled lazily. take() on an
infinite generator therefore still answers correctly; a generator with side effects
runs them 100000 times, and one that needs more than 100000 elements is truncated.
Reflection and the collections API beyond the calls above are also out of scope.

REGULAR EXPRESSIONS are genuine, over the shared engine in languages/lib/regex.js.
Kotlin has no regexp literal, so a pattern arrives as Regex(p), Regex(p, options) or
p.toRegex(options); the options are RegexOption entries, singly or in a setOf(...).
IGNORE_CASE, DOT_MATCHES_ALL, MULTILINE and COMMENTS map onto the engine's neutral
i / s / m / x, LITERAL quotes the pattern instead of being a mode, and CANON_EQ and
UNIX_LINES are REPORTED as unsupported rather than silently ignored. Regex answers
matches (whole input), containsMatchIn, find, findAll, matchEntire, replace (with a
string template or a lambda), replaceFirst, split with an optional limit, pattern and
toString; Regex.escape and Regex.escapeReplacement are the quoting helpers. A
MatchResult carries value, range, groupValues, groups (by number and by name),
destructured for val (a, b) = m.destructured, and next(). String.matches, .replace,
.split and .contains take a Regex too, and only then - a String argument keeps the
plain behavior. The replacement template is Kotlin's own dollar-one / dollar-brace-name
spelling, rewritten here to what the engine expands. The engine has no lookbehind, no
POSIX bracket expressions and no unicode property escapes, and Regex.escape answers
explicitly backslashed text rather than the JVM's quote-region form.

Beyond the executable subset the grammar aims at RECOGNITION: backtick-quoted names,
package and import declarations anywhere (and a package with a body), `private
constructor`, context parameters, contracts, intersection types, collection literals,
annotations wherever Kotlin allows them, and the trailing-comma and recovery shapes of
the compiler's own PSI test data all parse. Whatever is left - a class body the member
grammar cannot cover, an extension delegated property, a two-receiver form the model
cannot bind - is still PARSED and then reported as not implemented, so a whole Kotlin
file reads and -warn-unsupported lets the rest run.

The entry point is fun main(); self checking programs end with exitProcess(failures).") ;


// --- main rules

// A Kotlin name: either backtick-quoted (`class`, `1M`, `one two` - the escaped form
// that lets a keyword or an otherwise illegal spelling be used as a name) or a plain
// identifier. Every production below names it KId rather than the shared Id, so the
// quoted form is accepted everywhere a name may appear.
KId         = IdBacktick | NotHardKw Id ;
// A zero-width guard that fails when the word at the position is one of Kotlin's HARD
// keywords - the ones that can never be a bare name (Kotlin makes you write `class`
// in backticks, which IdBacktick above still accepts). Without it every keyword the
// surrounding rule failed to consume degrades into an identifier, and a genuinely
// broken file - `val v: package`, `abstract val a : package.Int` - parses as if the
// keyword were a type name. `this` and `super` are deliberately absent: this subset
// reads a bare `this` as an ordinary name.
NotHardKw   = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var kw = ["package", "class", "interface", "object", "typealias", "fun",
                  "val", "var", "if", "else", "when", "while", "do", "for", "return",
                  "break", "continue", "is", "in", "as", "throw", "try", "catch",
                  "finally", "typeof"]
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13 || cc === 12) { i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
        var w = ""
        var ch = c.peek(i)
        while ((ch >= 97 && ch <= 122) || (ch >= 65 && ch <= 90) || ch === 95 || (ch >= 48 && ch <= 57)) {
            w = w + String.fromCharCode(ch)
            i++
            ch = c.peek(i)
        }
        var k = 0
        while (k < kw.length) {
            if (kw[k] === w) { return abnf.newToken("\x01hard-keyword", 0) }
            k++
        }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// Whitespace and comments. These are spelled out here instead of including
// lib/cstyle-comments.abnf because Kotlin's whitespace set is not C's: the form feed
// (0x0c) is whitespace in Kotlin, and the lexer's own test data proves it (testData/
// lexer/kotlin/whitespaceCharacters/pageBreak lexes it into WHITE_SPACE). The same
// test data also proves which characters are NOT whitespace, so they stay out: the
// vertical tab (0x0b), the next line (0x85) and the line/paragraph separators
// (0x2028/0x2029) all lex to BAD_CHARACTER, i.e. Kotlin rejects those files. The lib
// file is shared with ~15 other grammars, where a form feed is equally harmless but
// the rest would be wrong, so the divergence lives here rather than there.
Whitespace  = { @+" \t\r\n\x0c" | Comment } ;
Comment     = LineComment | BlockComment ;
LineComment = "//" :whitespace() { NotNewline } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
BlockComment = KDocOk "/*" :whitespace() { NestedComment | NotStar | "*" { "*" } NotStarSlash } "*" { "*" } "/" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A KDoc comment is NOT trivia to Kotlin's parser: `/** ... */` is parsed, and the
// errors it finds are ordinary PsiErrorElements in the file's tree. This grammar lexes
// the whole comment away, so every such error was invisible and three corpus files
// whose ONLY defect is inside a doc comment were wrongly accepted.
//
// A full KDoc sub-parser is not what those three files need. Every error element in
// them is the same one - a qualified name with a DANGLING DOT, `Identifier expected` -
// in the two places KDoc parses a name:
//
//     [A.]   [O.]   [A.B.C...]      a markdown link
//     @see A.        @see O.        the subject of a name-taking tag
//
// so the guard recognizes exactly that and lets the comment fail to match, which makes
// the `/**` reach the code rules and the file be refused. It is deliberately narrow in
// the two directions that would cost the parse row:
//   - a bracket run whose content holds a SPACE, or does not START with an identifier
//     character, is markdown TEXT and not a link at all. Kotlin's own tree records
//     `[top level]`, `[O.with space]` and `[....]` as KDOC_TEXT, with no error.
//   - a bare `@see` with no subject is legal (psi/kdoc/SeeEmpty.txt gives it a KDOC_TAG
//     with no link), so an empty name run is not an error.
// Measured over the whole vendored corpus, the two patterns occur in exactly the three
// files Kotlin rejects and nowhere else.
KDocOk      = :script(~~
    (function() {
        if (c.peek(0) !== 47 || c.peek(1) !== 42 || c.peek(2) !== 42) { return undefined }
        var tags = ["see", "throws", "exception", "sample", "param", "property"]
        var i = 3
        var steps = 0
        var bad = false
        while (steps < 20000) {
            steps++
            var ch = c.peek(i)
            if (ch < 0) { break }
            if (ch === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { break }
            if (ch === 91) {
                // A bracket run, up to the closing `]` on the SAME line.
                var j = i + 1
                var body = ""
                var n = 0
                var cc = c.peek(j)
                while (cc >= 0 && cc !== 93 && cc !== 10 && n < 256) {
                    body = body + String.fromCharCode(cc)
                    j++
                    n++
                    cc = c.peek(j)
                }
                if (cc === 93 && body.length > 0) {
                    var f = body.charCodeAt(0)
                    var isName = (f >= 97 && f <= 122) || (f >= 65 && f <= 90) || f === 95 || f === 96
                    var spaced = body.indexOf(" ") >= 0 || body.indexOf("\t") >= 0
                    if (isName && !spaced && body.charCodeAt(body.length - 1) === 46) { bad = true }
                }
                if (bad) { break }
                i = j
                continue
            }
            if (ch === 64) {
                // A tag. Only the ones whose subject KDoc parses as a NAME are checked.
                var k = i + 1
                var w = ""
                var wn = 0
                var wc = c.peek(k)
                while (((wc >= 97 && wc <= 122) || (wc >= 65 && wc <= 90)) && wn < 24) {
                    w = w + String.fromCharCode(wc)
                    k++
                    wn++
                    wc = c.peek(k)
                }
                var known = false
                var t = 0
                while (t < tags.length) {
                    if (tags[t] === w) { known = true }
                    t++
                }
                if (known) {
                    while (c.peek(k) === 32 || c.peek(k) === 9) { k++ }
                    var name = ""
                    var nn = 0
                    var nc = c.peek(k)
                    while (nn < 256 && ((nc >= 97 && nc <= 122) || (nc >= 65 && nc <= 90) || (nc >= 48 && nc <= 57) || nc === 95 || nc === 46 || nc === 96)) {
                        name = name + String.fromCharCode(nc)
                        k++
                        nn++
                        nc = c.peek(k)
                    }
                    if (name.length > 0 && name.charCodeAt(name.length - 1) === 46) { bad = true; break }
                }
                i = k
                continue
            }
            i++
        }
        if (bad) { return abnf.newToken("\x01kdoc-dangling-name", 0) }
    })()
~~) ;
// Kotlin block comments NEST - /*/**/*/ is ONE comment. The nested form is its own
// rule so that it carries no :whitespace command: running one inside a comment
// would switch skipping back on halfway through the comment being skipped.
NestedComment = "/*" { NestedComment | NotStar | "*" { "*" } NotStarSlash } "*" { "*" } "/" ;
NotNewline  = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStar     = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStarSlash = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\x2e" | "\x30"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// Package and import are also declaration alternatives, not just a header: a source
// set concatenated into one file (the `// FILE: x.kt` convention of the Kotlin
// compiler's own test data) carries several of each, interleaved with declarations.
// A file: an optional package directive, the import list, then the declarations. The
// `[ Modifiers ]` in front of the package directive is Kotlin's own doing - it parses
// `public @[a] package name` and reports the modifiers as an error afterwards
// (psi/packages/PackageModifiers).
Program     = { Annotation } [ [ Modifiers ] Package ] { Import } { TopDecl } ;
// One top-level declaration. Factored out of Program so a `package name { ... }` block
// (the pre-1.0 package syntax that the PSI corpus still carries) can hold the same set.
// A `package` directive and the `import` list are NOT in here: Kotlin allows them only
// at the head of a file, and letting them recur mid-file is what made the 37 `// FILE:`
// corpus files - several compilation units, sometimes several LANGUAGES, concatenated
// into one .kt by the test harness - parse as though they were one legal file.
TopDecl     = [ Modifiers ] ( Interface | ObjectDecl | EnumClass | AnnotationClass | TypeAlias | ClassDecl | FunDecl <~~ registerFun(pop()) ~~> | ExtFun | ExtProp | UnsupFun | PropAccessor | TopVisProp | TopProp | TopBareProp ) ;

// Modifiers/annotations before a declaration are parsed and ignored. Annotations
// accept ALL forms: an argument list @Suppress("Unused"), qualified names @a.b.C,
// use-site targets @field:A / @get:A / @file:A, and annotation arrays @[A B C(x)].
// Body-shaping modifiers (enum/annotation/companion) are not consumed - those
// declaration forms route to notImplemented instead.
Modifiers   = { Modifier | CompanionMod | Annotation | ContextClause | AnnEnumMod | VarianceMod } ;
// `annotation` and `enum` are the two soft keywords that also START a declaration
// (annotation class / enum class), so they cannot sit in the plain Modifier list - a
// greedy run would eat the keyword the declaration form needs (see docs/abnf-dialect-
// gotchas.md, "A greedy modifier list eats the declaration keyword"). They ARE ordinary
// modifiers everywhere else, and Kotlin's own testData leans on it: psi/SimpleModifiers.kt,
// psi/SoftKeywords.kt, psi/annotation/Annotations.kt and psi/annotation/options/java.kt
// all spell a multi-line modifier run with `annotation` or `enum` in the middle of it and
// carry a clean parse tree. The NotClassW guard is what keeps `annotation class Foo` and
// `enum class Kind` routed to their own productions: the word only counts as a modifier
// when `class` does NOT follow it.
AnnEnumMod  = ( "annotation" | "enum" ) KwEnd NotColon NotClassW ;
// `in` and `out` are variance modifiers, and Kotlin lets them stand in an ordinary
// declaration modifier run as well (psi/SimpleModifiers.kt, psi/SoftKeywords.kt and
// psi/annotation/Annotations.kt all end their multi-line run with `in` / `out` before
// the `class` keyword, all three with a clean parse tree). They are the two modifier
// words that are also HARD operators - `a in b`, `for (x in xs)`, `in 1..2 ->` - so they
// are only taken when a modifier, a declaration keyword or an annotation follows; that
// keeps every ordinary use of `in` out of the modifier list.
VarianceMod = ( "in" | "out" ) KwEnd ModWordAhead ;
// A zero-width guard that fails unless the next token starts a modifier run or a
// declaration - an `@` annotation, or one of the modifier / declaration keywords.
ModWordAhead = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var kws = "|public|private|protected|internal|open|final|abstract|sealed|data"
                + "|override|lateinit|const|inner|vararg|noinline|crossinline|tailrec"
                + "|operator|infix|inline|external|suspend|expect|actual|reified|value"
                + "|annotation|enum|companion|context|in|out|class|interface|object|fun"
                + "|val|var|typealias|constructor|init|"
        var i = 0
        var steps = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (steps < 100000) {
            steps++
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13 || cc === 12) { i++; cc = c.peek(i); continue }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                i = i + 2
                cc = c.peek(i)
                while (cc >= 0 && cc !== 10 && steps < 100000) { steps++; i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
                continue
            }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 42) {
                i = i + 2
                while (steps < 100000) {
                    steps++
                    var d = c.peek(i)
                    if (d < 0) { break }
                    if (d === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) { i = i + 2; break }
                    i++
                }
                cc = c.peek(i)
                continue
            }
            break
        }
        if (cc === 64) { return undefined }
        var w = ""
        var k = 0
        while (k < 24) {
            var b2 = c.peek(i + k)
            var isWord = (b2 >= 48 && b2 <= 57) || (b2 >= 65 && b2 <= 90) || (b2 >= 97 && b2 <= 122) || b2 === 95
            if (!isWord) { break }
            w = w + String.fromCharCode(b2)
            k++
        }
        return (w !== "" && kws.indexOf("|" + w + "|") >= 0)
            ? undefined
            : abnf.newToken("\x01no-modifier-follows", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// A zero-width guard that fails when the next word is `class` (the twin of the guard of
// the same name in kotlin-to-llvm-ir.abnf).
NotClassW   = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13) { i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
        return (cc === 99 && c.peek(i + 1) === 108 && c.peek(i + 2) === 97
                && c.peek(i + 3) === 115 && c.peek(i + 4) === 115)
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01class-follows", 0)
            : undefined
    })()
~~) ;
// `companion fun String.f() {}` / `companion val Foo.p get() = 1` (the CompanionBlocks
// / CompanionExtensions proposal the PSI corpus carries): `companion` acts as a plain
// declaration modifier there. It is NOT noted like the other modifiers - nothing reads
// it - and the NotObjectKw guard keeps it away from `companion object`, which is a
// declaration FORM with its own production rather than a modified one.
CompanionMod = "companion" KwEnd NotColon NotBrace NotObjectKw ;
// A context-receiver / context-parameter list, `context(Logger, Tx) fun f() { ... }`.
// Parsed and dropped like any other declaration modifier. The `(` is required, so an
// ordinary identifier named `context` is never mistaken for the keyword.
// The entries are NOT individually optional. Written as `[ CtxParam ] { "," [ CtxParam ] }`
// the list accepted every shape of psi/contextParameters/trailingCommaForbidden.kt -
// `context(s: String,,)`, `context(,)`, `context(, s: String)` - each of which Kotlin
// reports as "Type expected". A single TRAILING comma stays allowed (Kotlin's list
// trailing-comma rule); only an EMPTY entry is refused.
ContextClause = "context" KwEnd "(" [ CtxParam { "," CtxParam } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
// A context parameter is an optional name followed by its type. A LABEL is not a name
// here: `fun foo(f: context(label@String) () -> Unit)` is refused by Kotlin's own
// parser, which records `Expecting comma or ')'` at the `@` and then loses the rest of
// the file (psi/contextParameters/labelInFunctionTypeContext.txt). An earlier `"@"`
// alternative existed solely to accept that one file and cost the reject row for it.
// The modifiers are what `context(noinline a: () -> Int)` needs (psi/contracts/good/
// holdsIn/withContextParameters.kt); they are parsed and dropped like every other
// context-clause part.
CtxParam    = { Annotation | ParamMod } [ KId ":" ] Type ;
// The trailing NotColon lets a soft-keyword modifier double as an identifier when it
// is actually a name: a real modifier is never immediately followed by `:`, but a
// parameter or property named `data`/`value`/`inner` is (data: Intent?). Without the
// guard [Modifiers] would eat the name and strand the `:`.
// A matched modifier is recorded in the walk-time pendingMods list (it cannot be
// pushed: the enclosing Program/Member repetition has no tag to collect it). Each
// declaration takes the list with takeMods() at its OWN keyword, which is parsed
// directly after the modifiers - so `data class` and `inner class` see `data`/`inner`
// while a member's modifiers cannot leak into the class being built around it.
Modifier    = ( "public" | "private" | "protected" | "internal" | "open" | "final"
              | "abstract" | "sealed" | "data" | "override" | "lateinit" | "const"
              | "inner" | "vararg" | "noinline" | "crossinline" | "tailrec"
              | "operator" | "infix" | "inline" | "external" | "suspend"
              | "expect" | "actual" | "reified" | "value" ) <~~ noteMod(up.in) ~~> KwEnd NotColon ;
Annotation  = "@" [ AnnTarget ":" ] ( AnnSet | AnnEntry ) ;
// The longer name comes FIRST wherever one target is a prefix of another
// (setparam/set): the ordered choice commits to its first match, and the KwEnd that
// follows the group can only reject the whole alternative, not retry inside it.
AnnTarget   = ( "field" | "property" | "get" | "setparam" | "set" | "receiver"
              | "param" | "delegate" | "file" | "all" ) KwEnd ;
AnnSet      = "[" { AnnEntry } "]" ;
// Each segment may carry type arguments - `@[foo<A, B>(a, b) foo.bar.doo<f>.foo<bar, goo>.foo]`
// in psi/annotation/Annotations.kt is one annotation set with a clean parse tree - so the
// angle run is skipped after every name, not only after the last.
AnnEntry    = KId [ SkipAngle ] { "." KId [ SkipAngle ] } [ AnnArgsOk SkipParen NotArrow ] ;
// SkipParen consumes any balanced run, so every shape of psi/emptyArgumentsInAnnotations.kt
// - `@Ann(1, ,)`, `@Ann(,)`, `@Ann(x =)`, `@Ann(*, 2)` - was swallowed whole where Kotlin
// reports "Expecting an argument". This zero-width guard walks the run the skip is about
// to eat and refuses it when a depth-1 argument is EMPTY (nothing between two commas, or
// between the `(` and the first comma), or ends in a dangling `=` (a named argument with
// no value) or a lone `*` (a spread with no operand). `@Ann()` and a trailing comma stay
// legal, since both are ordinary Kotlin.
// It is a guard rather than a real argument grammar because annotation arguments are
// parsed and dropped here; the only question asked is whether the run is well formed.
AnnArgsOk   = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var steps = 0
        var ch = c.peek(i)
        while ((ch === 32 || ch === 9 || ch === 10 || ch === 13 || ch === 12) && steps < 100000) {
            steps++; i++; ch = c.peek(i)
        }
        if (c.peek(i) !== 40) { return undefined }
        i++
        var depth = 1
        var last = 40
        var bad = false
        while (steps < 200000) {
            steps++
            var b = c.peek(i)
            if (b < 0) { break }
            if (b === 32 || b === 9 || b === 10 || b === 13 || b === 12) { i++ }
